WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.475
Block three.

2
00:00:01.475 --> 00:00:05.675
From single chains to interactions, and generative models.

3
00:00:05.675 --> 00:00:12.700
On the page, this block is the sections "From single chains to interactions" and "Generative structure modelling".

4
00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:14.325
Three things.

5
00:00:14.325 --> 00:00:22.425
Complexes: proteins do not work alone, and predicting how two chains fit together is a harder problem with its own failure modes.

6
00:00:22.425 --> 00:00:26.350
Then diffusion, which is how generative models work.

7
00:00:26.350 --> 00:00:30.250
And then design: making proteins that do not exist.

8
00:00:30.250 --> 00:00:38.050
And a question running underneath all of it, which I will return to at the end: when a model tells you something, what have you actually learned?

9
00:00:38.650 --> 00:00:44.200
Proteins work in complexes, so predicting a single chain is only half the job.

10
00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:47.625
The coevolution argument extends naturally.

11
00:00:47.625 --> 00:00:57.925
If two proteins interact, residues at their interface are under the same kind of pressure as residues in a core: a change on one side has to be accommodated on the other.

12
00:00:57.925 --> 00:01:03.525
So interfaces coevolve too, and the signal should be visible across the two families.

13
00:01:03.525 --> 00:01:07.050
There is a complication, and it is the hard part.

14
00:01:07.050 --> 00:01:13.375
For a single chain, the alignment is straightforward: all these sequences are versions of this protein.

15
00:01:13.375 --> 00:01:21.475
For two proteins you need to know which sequence in family A interacts with which sequence in family B, across all the organisms.

16
00:01:21.475 --> 00:01:25.525
Pair them wrongly and the coevolution signal is destroyed.

17
00:01:25.525 --> 00:01:33.475
In bacteria you can often use the fact that interacting genes sit next to each other in the genome; in eukaryotes it is harder.

18
00:01:33.475 --> 00:01:35.350
Two approaches.

19
00:01:35.350 --> 00:01:45.375
FoldDock showed that AlphaFold2, trained only on single chains, can predict complexes if you simply join the chains together with a gap and feed in paired alignments.

20
00:01:45.375 --> 00:01:50.550
That it works at all is interesting: the network had never been shown a complex.

21
00:01:50.550 --> 00:01:54.575
And AlphaFold-Multimer, which was retrained for the job.

22
00:01:55.175 --> 00:02:02.350
And complexes need their own confidence scores, because the single-chain ones do not answer the question you care about.

23
00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:16.825
From the model itself: ipTM, the interface predicted TM-score, which AlphaFold-Multimer reports; and pDockQ, used by FoldDock, which is computed from the pLDDT at the interface and the number of contacts across it.

24
00:02:16.825 --> 00:02:26.300
Against a known true structure: DockQ, running from nought to one, which is the standard measure of how well a predicted complex matches an experimental one.

25
00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:34.200
And the point that connects to the previous block: each chain can be perfectly predicted while their placement relative to each other is wrong.

26
00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:40.275
That is precisely the PAE picture again, now between chains rather than between domains.

27
00:02:40.275 --> 00:02:50.050
So when you assess a complex, look at the PAE block between the two chains, not at their individual pLDDTs, which may both be excellent and irrelevant.

28
00:02:50.650 --> 00:02:55.625
This is one of the most important slides in the session, and it is a short one.

29
00:02:55.625 --> 00:03:00.925
A plausible predicted complex is not evidence that two proteins interact.

30
00:03:00.925 --> 00:03:03.425
Think about what the model does.

31
00:03:03.425 --> 00:03:06.950
You give it two chains and ask it to put them together.

32
00:03:06.950 --> 00:03:08.975
It puts them together.

33
00:03:08.975 --> 00:03:20.175
It is not asked, and does not answer, whether these two proteins are ever in the same place at the same time in a cell, or whether that interaction happens under any physiological condition.

34
00:03:20.175 --> 00:03:25.425
So a structure predictor can suggest how two supplied molecules could interact.

35
00:03:25.425 --> 00:03:28.300
It cannot establish whether they do.

36
00:03:28.300 --> 00:03:31.800
Combined with other evidence, it becomes powerful.

37
00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:47.250
The example on the slide is a good one: all-against-all modelling of fourteen fertilisation proteins pointed towards a pentameric complex of sperm IZUMO1, SPACA6 and TMEM81 with egg JUNO and CD9.

38
00:03:47.250 --> 00:03:53.725
That is a real hypothesis about a real process, generated computationally and then pursued.

39
00:03:53.725 --> 00:03:57.325
But the structure was the hypothesis, not the result.

40
00:03:57.925 --> 00:04:03.925
Now a failure mode that is specific to these tools, and which I think is under-appreciated.

41
00:04:03.925 --> 00:04:10.475
Run a complex prediction five times with different random seeds and you get five different answers.

42
00:04:10.475 --> 00:04:14.550
So the question becomes: can you tell which of them is right?

43
00:04:14.550 --> 00:04:17.600
Here is the experiment, from the notebook.

44
00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:20.100
Five seeds per complex.

45
00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:29.400
For every complex tested, the seed with the highest ipTM is a good model, with DockQ between nought point six one and nought point eight five.

46
00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:31.725
That sounds encouraging.

47
00:04:31.725 --> 00:04:38.600
But: for the antibody D one point three, three of the five seeds put the antibody in completely the wrong place.

48
00:04:38.600 --> 00:04:48.900
And worse, correct models of a Fab bound to MHC class one score ipTM nought point seven three, while wrong D one point three models score nought point seven eight.

49
00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:51.925
A higher confidence on a worse answer.

50
00:04:51.925 --> 00:04:57.800
So no single ipTM threshold separates right from wrong across different complexes.

51
00:04:57.800 --> 00:05:11.225
And the same thing at larger scale: on a hundred and ten antibody-antigen complexes that were not in training, sampling two hundred models raised the DockQ of the best model from below nought point three to above nought point five.

52
00:05:11.225 --> 00:05:13.650
The good answer is in there.

53
00:05:13.650 --> 00:05:17.000
But the confidence score often cannot pick it out.

54
00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:23.300
So sampling quality and ranking quality are two different things, and the field is better at the first.

55
00:05:23.300 --> 00:05:28.400
Practically: generate many models rather than one, and be sceptical of the ranking.

56
00:05:28.400 --> 00:05:46.400
Two refinements worth knowing: trimming a construct to just the interacting domains raises ipTM for the same interface, which tells you the score is partly about the rest of the protein; and ipSAE, from twenty twenty-five, counts only well-placed pairs rather than averaging over everything.

57
00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:57.025
This slide is a rough guide to when a complex prediction is worth believing, and I want to be clear that it is a heuristic from this lecture, not an established classification.

58
00:05:57.025 --> 00:06:00.375
Treat it as a way of thinking rather than a rule.

59
00:06:00.375 --> 00:06:10.025
Broadly, confidence is well-founded when the interaction is evolutionarily conserved, both partners have deep alignments, and the interface is large and rigid.

60
00:06:10.025 --> 00:06:24.175
It is least reliable when the interface is small, when one partner is flexible or disordered, when the alignment is shallow, and when there are competing partners, so that the model is asked which of several plausible arrangements is the real one.

61
00:06:24.175 --> 00:06:31.550
That last case is worth dwelling on: proteasome paralogs, or IZUMO1 with its several competing partners.

62
00:06:31.550 --> 00:06:36.475
The model will produce one answer, confidently, where biology has several.

63
00:06:36.475 --> 00:06:45.925
In the lab each group predicts one complex from each region on the AlphaFold Server and checks whether the confidence actually tracks how hard the problem is.

64
00:06:45.925 --> 00:06:51.850
The targets are one S H W, five F four E, eight T Q seven and one Y C R.

65
00:06:52.450 --> 00:06:57.300
Now we change direction: from predicting structures to generating them.

66
00:06:57.300 --> 00:07:03.400
And the mechanism is diffusion, which is also what is behind the image generators you have seen.

67
00:07:03.400 --> 00:07:07.025
Three steps, and only the middle one involves learning.

68
00:07:07.025 --> 00:07:09.725
Step one, the forward process.

69
00:07:09.725 --> 00:07:13.975
Take a real example and add a small amount of Gaussian noise.

70
00:07:13.975 --> 00:07:16.150
Then add a little more.

71
00:07:16.150 --> 00:07:20.350
Then more again, for many steps, until nothing is left but noise.

72
00:07:20.350 --> 00:07:25.525
There is no learning here; it is just a recipe for destroying data gradually.

73
00:07:25.525 --> 00:07:27.550
Step two, training.

74
00:07:27.550 --> 00:07:34.600
Show the network a noisy example and tell it which step it came from, and ask it to predict the noise that was added.

75
00:07:34.600 --> 00:07:43.050
That is an ordinary supervised learning problem, with the Session nine training loop, and the labels are free because you added the noise yourself.

76
00:07:43.050 --> 00:07:45.425
Step three, generation.

77
00:07:45.425 --> 00:07:47.675
Start from pure noise.

78
00:07:47.675 --> 00:07:52.925
Ask the network what noise it sees, remove a little of it, and repeat, many times.

79
00:07:52.925 --> 00:07:59.925
What is left at the end is a new sample that looks like the training data but is not any particular example of it.

80
00:07:59.925 --> 00:08:07.550
The trick, and the reason this works better than trying to generate in one shot, is that each step is a small, easy correction.

81
00:08:07.550 --> 00:08:16.125
You are never asking the model to produce a whole protein out of nothing; you are asking it to make something slightly less noisy, hundreds of times.

82
00:08:16.125 --> 00:08:28.750
Two relatives worth naming: flow matching, which learns a smooth path from noise to data and needs fewer steps, and the variational autoencoder, which samples from a learned low-dimensional space instead.

83
00:08:29.350 --> 00:08:34.825
The notebook does this on the smallest possible example, so that you can see the whole thing.

84
00:08:34.825 --> 00:08:38.400
The data is a thousand points arranged in a ring.

85
00:08:38.400 --> 00:08:45.000
Train a small network to predict the noise, then start from pure noise and run a hundred denoising steps.

86
00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:46.925
Out comes a ring.

87
00:08:46.925 --> 00:08:51.550
Not the same thousand points; a new set of points with the same shape.

88
00:08:51.550 --> 00:09:02.375
And the training behaves as you would expect from Session nine: the noise-prediction error falls from nought point nine three nine at step one to nought point six three eight by step two thousand.

89
00:09:02.375 --> 00:09:07.175
The reason I like this toy is that it makes the claim concrete and modest.

90
00:09:07.175 --> 00:09:13.025
The model has learned the shape of the data distribution, well enough to draw new samples from it.

91
00:09:13.025 --> 00:09:19.075
Nothing more mystical than that is happening when the same procedure runs on protein coordinates.

92
00:09:19.075 --> 00:09:28.025
And the third discussion problem asks the right question about it: has such a model learned the distribution, or has it memorised its training examples?

93
00:09:28.025 --> 00:09:34.775
That question matters a great deal for protein design, where you want to know whether a generated protein is new.

94
00:09:35.375 --> 00:09:38.950
Briefly, AlphaFold3, and what is different.

95
00:09:38.950 --> 00:09:48.000
It predicts all atoms at once, for any molecule made of atoms: proteins, nucleic acids, small molecules, ions, modified residues.

96
00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:53.275
So complexes with DNA or a drug are in scope, which they were not before.

97
00:09:53.275 --> 00:10:00.275
And it generates coordinates with a diffusion module, rather than the geometric structure module of AlphaFold2.

98
00:10:00.275 --> 00:10:08.475
That brings a cost, and it is the one from two slides ago: sampling means several different answers and the ranking problem comes back.

99
00:10:08.475 --> 00:10:22.425
It also means the model can hallucinate plausible structure in regions that are actually disordered, because a generative model's job is to produce something that looks like the training data, and disordered regions do not look like anything.

100
00:10:22.425 --> 00:10:35.250
And the honest assessment from the blind test: in CASP sixteen, AlphaFold3 was slightly better for protein complexes, and that difference disappeared when AlphaFold2 was given massive sampling.

101
00:10:35.250 --> 00:10:39.600
So it is an advance in scope more than a step change in accuracy.

102
00:10:40.200 --> 00:10:45.425
A slide about how we know any of this, which I think belongs in a course like this.

103
00:10:45.425 --> 00:10:49.100
AlphaFold3 was published without training code.

104
00:10:49.100 --> 00:10:53.950
Open reproductions followed, and that is how the community checks things.

105
00:10:53.950 --> 00:10:56.450
Compare two kinds of claim.

106
00:10:56.450 --> 00:11:12.000
ESMFold2 is a frozen six-billion-parameter language model plus an AlphaFold3-style diffusion module, about seven billion parameters, released under the MIT licence: you can run it, test it on your own data, and find out where it fails.

107
00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:15.400
OpenDDE is released under Apache.

108
00:11:15.400 --> 00:11:17.950
These are results you can check.

109
00:11:17.950 --> 00:11:32.000
Then there are companies reporting large gains, with their biggest improvements claimed exactly where AlphaFold3 is weakest, on examples unlike the training set, with no released code, no model and no independent evaluation.

110
00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:34.600
That is a claim, not a result.

111
00:11:34.600 --> 00:11:36.750
It may well be true.

112
00:11:36.750 --> 00:11:41.500
You cannot know, and neither can they, in the sense that matters scientifically.

113
00:11:41.500 --> 00:11:48.700
That distinction is not cynicism; it is the difference between a field that can correct itself and one that cannot.

114
00:11:48.700 --> 00:11:54.875
And it applies to your own work: a method nobody else can run is a method nobody else can check.

115
00:11:54.875 --> 00:12:04.950
One more honest note, from our own group's work: for RNA, current methods largely recognise recurring motifs rather than generalising to new RNA folds.

116
00:12:04.950 --> 00:12:08.500
The protein success has not simply carried over.

117
00:12:09.100 --> 00:12:16.750
Now design, which is the other half of the twenty twenty-four Nobel Prize, and the pipeline is three steps plus an experiment.

118
00:12:16.750 --> 00:12:18.825
Generate a backbone.

119
00:12:18.825 --> 00:12:21.725
Choose a sequence that will fold into it.

120
00:12:21.725 --> 00:12:24.500
Check computationally that it does.

121
00:12:24.500 --> 00:12:27.275
Then test it in the laboratory.

122
00:12:27.275 --> 00:12:33.375
And I want to flag the third step before we get there, because it is where most misunderstandings live.

123
00:12:33.375 --> 00:12:42.650
Refolding your designed sequence with AlphaFold and finding that it gives back the backbone you started from is a consistency check between two models.

124
00:12:42.650 --> 00:12:44.875
It is not validation.

125
00:12:44.875 --> 00:12:49.700
Both models can be wrong in the same direction, and they share training data.

126
00:12:49.700 --> 00:12:54.225
The validation is the last step, and it is still experimental.

127
00:12:54.225 --> 00:13:09.200
Hundreds of RFdiffusion designs have been tested in the laboratory, and the one I would point at is a binder against influenza haemagglutinin whose cryo-electron microscopy structure was, in the authors' words, nearly identical to the design model.

128
00:13:09.200 --> 00:13:12.550
That is what a validated design looks like.

129
00:13:13.150 --> 00:13:19.150
Before the design tools themselves, a conceptual problem that the whole field is grappling with.

130
00:13:19.150 --> 00:13:21.925
A protein is not one structure.

131
00:13:21.925 --> 00:13:23.650
Loops move.

132
00:13:23.650 --> 00:13:25.525
Domains swing.

133
00:13:25.525 --> 00:13:36.125
Many proteins switch between distinct states: haemoglobin's T and R states from Session six are the textbook case, and the whole point of haemoglobin is that it changes shape.

134
00:13:36.125 --> 00:13:43.750
AlphaFold gives you one structure, and says nothing about which state it is, or how likely that state is relative to others.

135
00:13:43.750 --> 00:13:51.425
Molecular dynamics is honest about this, since it produces a trajectory, but it is far too slow, as Block one said.

136
00:13:51.425 --> 00:13:55.150
So three attempts to get an ensemble out of a predictor.

137
00:13:55.150 --> 00:14:08.875
AFsample samples the same network very hard: it leaves dropout on at prediction time, which is deliberately the opposite of Session nine's rule, and generates around six thousand models per target instead of twenty-five.

138
00:14:08.875 --> 00:14:12.675
It topped CASP fifteen for protein assemblies.

139
00:14:12.675 --> 00:14:20.950
AlphaFlow retrains AlphaFold as a generative model with flow matching, so each run draws a different structure from a distribution.

140
00:14:20.950 --> 00:14:36.575
And BioEmu is a learned shortcut for molecular dynamics, trained on more than two hundred milliseconds of simulation plus stability data, producing thousands of structures per hour on one GPU and free energies to within about one kilocalorie per mole.

141
00:14:36.575 --> 00:14:43.550
And the caution, which I think is the right note: a set of models is only an ensemble if the proportions are right.

142
00:14:43.550 --> 00:14:51.300
If a protein spends ninety per cent of its time in state A, a good ensemble should give you state A ninety per cent of the time.

143
00:14:51.300 --> 00:14:55.100
Checking that is much harder than checking a single structure.

144
00:14:55.100 --> 00:14:58.675
Variety is not the same as the right variety.

145
00:14:59.275 --> 00:15:03.225
Step one of the pipeline: where does a new backbone come from?

146
00:15:03.225 --> 00:15:10.825
RFdiffusion applies exactly the diffusion procedure we built on the ring, with protein coordinates instead of points.

147
00:15:10.825 --> 00:15:19.000
Start from residues placed at random, and denoise repeatedly, using a fine-tuned RoseTTAFold to do the denoising.

148
00:15:19.000 --> 00:15:25.200
Out comes a protein backbone that looks like a real fold and is not any particular existing one.

149
00:15:25.200 --> 00:15:28.800
Unconditional generation is a curiosity.

150
00:15:28.800 --> 00:15:35.475
What makes it useful is conditioning: holding part of the problem fixed while the rest is generated around it.

151
00:15:35.475 --> 00:15:41.175
Hold a target protein's surface fixed, and generate a binder shaped to fit against it.

152
00:15:41.175 --> 00:15:51.625
Or hold a functional motif fixed, say the three residues of an active site in their correct relative geometry, and build a stable scaffold around them that holds them in place.

153
00:15:51.625 --> 00:15:55.375
That second one is a genuinely new capability.

154
00:15:55.375 --> 00:16:00.400
The classical approach was to search known folds for one that could host your motif.

155
00:16:00.400 --> 00:16:02.950
This builds a fold to order.

156
00:16:03.550 --> 00:16:07.625
Step two, and it is the inverse of everything else in this course.

157
00:16:07.625 --> 00:16:11.050
Normally: sequence given, structure unknown.

158
00:16:11.050 --> 00:16:14.250
Here: structure given, sequence unknown.

159
00:16:14.250 --> 00:16:18.100
Which amino acid sequence will fold into this backbone?

160
00:16:18.100 --> 00:16:23.375
That is called inverse folding, and it turns out to be much easier than folding.

161
00:16:23.375 --> 00:16:35.275
ProteinMPNN treats the backbone as a graph: each residue is a node, connected to its nearest neighbours in space rather than in sequence, which is the right notion of neighbour for this problem.

162
00:16:35.275 --> 00:16:39.400
An encoder lets each residue read its structural environment.

163
00:16:39.400 --> 00:16:49.325
Then a decoder chooses amino acids one at a time, conditioned on the structure and on the choices already made, exactly as a language model writes text.

164
00:16:49.325 --> 00:16:57.400
The headline number: fifty-two point four per cent native sequence recovery, against thirty-two point nine per cent for Rosetta.

165
00:16:57.400 --> 00:17:03.475
That is, when given a real backbone, it proposes the real amino acid more than half the time.

166
00:17:03.475 --> 00:17:10.950
Note that recovering the native sequence is a proxy: a different sequence may fold just as well, and sometimes better.

167
00:17:10.950 --> 00:17:19.050
And it has a practical virtue that made it widely used immediately: it rescued designs that had previously failed in the laboratory.

168
00:17:19.050 --> 00:17:24.725
The same backbones, given better sequences, became proteins that actually folded.

169
00:17:24.725 --> 00:17:36.450
The randomness comes from sampling each amino acid from its predicted probabilities with a temperature, so the same backbone gives you different sequences on each run, and you can make many and test several.

170
00:17:37.050 --> 00:17:42.450
And now a trick I find genuinely elegant, which closes a loop with Session nine.

171
00:17:42.450 --> 00:17:45.275
AlphaFold2 is differentiable.

172
00:17:45.275 --> 00:17:48.325
Which means you can backpropagate through it.

173
00:17:48.325 --> 00:17:52.950
Session nine used that to change the weights while the input stayed fixed.

174
00:17:52.950 --> 00:17:55.475
BindCraft does the opposite.

175
00:17:55.475 --> 00:17:58.425
Freeze the weights, and change the input.

176
00:17:58.425 --> 00:18:10.250
Concretely: represent the binder's sequence as undecided, a weight for each amino acid at each position, and define the score as AlphaFold's confidence that this binder binds the target.

177
00:18:10.250 --> 00:18:16.975
Then run gradient descent on the sequence itself, until AlphaFold is as confident as it can be made.

178
00:18:16.975 --> 00:18:20.350
You are optimising the question rather than the model.

179
00:18:20.350 --> 00:18:30.300
The idea started as hallucination, around twenty twenty-one: optimise a sequence until a predictor is confident it folds into something, and see what you get.

180
00:18:30.300 --> 00:18:45.300
BindCraft applies it to binders, and reports experimental success rates between ten and a hundred per cent, with no high-throughput screening, against targets including cell-surface receptors, allergens, de novo designed proteins and Cas9.

181
00:18:45.300 --> 00:18:50.525
Designed binders reduced IgE binding to birch allergen in patient samples.

182
00:18:50.525 --> 00:18:57.875
And the warning that applies to all three of these tools, and which I want to state clearly: they optimise against a predictor.

183
00:18:57.875 --> 00:19:03.250
A design that makes AlphaFold confident is a hypothesis about a protein.

184
00:19:03.250 --> 00:19:05.450
It is not a protein.

185
00:19:05.450 --> 00:19:09.500
Every single one of these papers ends in a laboratory.

186
00:19:10.100 --> 00:19:17.700
Where this stands now, with the results and the claims kept apart, because that distinction is the last thing I want to teach you.

187
00:19:17.700 --> 00:19:18.975
Results.

188
00:19:18.975 --> 00:19:24.500
The pipeline works: designed binders bind, in the laboratory, confirmed structurally.

189
00:19:24.500 --> 00:19:40.800
Bennett and colleagues in twenty twenty-six designed antibodies, including nanobodies and single-chain and full antibodies, against chosen epitopes, and cryo-electron microscopy confirmed the designed binding pose and the loop conformations at atomic accuracy.

190
00:19:40.800 --> 00:19:46.475
Designing an antibody against a chosen epitope was, very recently, not possible.

191
00:19:46.475 --> 00:19:56.650
And the honest part of that same paper: the first designs bound only modestly, and affinity maturation in the laboratory was needed to reach nanomolar binders.

192
00:19:56.650 --> 00:20:00.325
The experiment is still inside the loop, not after it.

193
00:20:00.325 --> 00:20:13.475
Boltz-2 predicts binding affinity and approaches physics-based free-energy perturbation on held-out benchmarks while being at least a thousand times faster, which, if it holds up, matters a great deal for drug discovery.

194
00:20:13.475 --> 00:20:14.775
Claims.

195
00:20:14.775 --> 00:20:21.150
Drug-design engines reporting large gains with no code, no model and no independent test.

196
00:20:21.150 --> 00:20:25.025
Treat them as claims until someone outside can check.

197
00:20:25.025 --> 00:20:29.225
And then the question on the slide: what is left for experiments?

198
00:20:29.225 --> 00:20:31.950
My answer, and you may disagree.

199
00:20:31.950 --> 00:20:36.375
Experiments decide whether a design works, which no model can.

200
00:20:36.375 --> 00:20:42.950
They measure what models cannot: affinity in a living cell, toxicity, whether anything happens at all.

201
00:20:42.950 --> 00:20:49.325
And they produce the data that the next model is trained on, which means the loop runs in both directions.

202
00:20:49.325 --> 00:20:55.200
A field that stopped doing experiments would stop improving its models within one generation.

203
00:20:55.800 --> 00:20:58.300
The last slide of the course.

204
00:20:58.300 --> 00:21:06.725
Modern structure prediction combines alignment and evolution with neural networks, attention, pair representations and geometry.

205
00:21:06.725 --> 00:21:10.350
Every piece of that sentence is a session you have done.

206
00:21:10.350 --> 00:21:16.725
And the line I would like you to leave with: a model is a hypothesis with a confidence estimate attached.

207
00:21:16.725 --> 00:21:19.125
It is not an experiment.

208
00:21:19.125 --> 00:21:31.600
That holds for AlphaFold's pLDDT, for a designed binder before anyone has expressed it, for a complex prediction that has not been tested, and for the predictor you built in your capstone project.

209
00:21:31.600 --> 00:21:36.800
It is not a reason for scepticism about these methods, which are extraordinary.

210
00:21:36.800 --> 00:21:42.275
It is the thing that lets you use them well: knowing what you have, and knowing what you do not.

211
00:21:42.875 --> 00:21:45.325
That is the end of the lectures.

212
00:21:45.325 --> 00:21:54.900
What remains is your capstone project: build a secondary-structure predictor, choose your input, your model and your test set, and report honestly what you got.

213
00:21:54.900 --> 00:22:05.300
Everything in this course is relevant to it, and Session seven's lesson is the one that will decide your result: the representation of the input matters as much as the model.

214
00:22:05.300 --> 00:22:09.300
Choose a test set with no homologs of your training data.

215
00:22:09.300 --> 00:22:12.250
Compare against a simple baseline.

216
00:22:12.250 --> 00:22:14.625
Report more than accuracy.

217
00:22:14.625 --> 00:22:19.200
And tell us what did not work, which is usually the more interesting half.

218
00:22:19.200 --> 00:22:28.050
The Session twelve page has all of this written out, and its "Check your understanding" section at the end is the fastest way to find out whether it stuck.

219
00:22:28.050 --> 00:22:32.100
The course notebook is linked in the box under the page's title.

220
00:22:32.100 --> 00:22:34.225
Thank you for this course.

221
00:22:34.225 --> 00:22:36.225
It has been a pleasure.

